{"author":{"id":"6d85cf2fcebaeefd8c5c528f2d5f5f98f26ce3275422d0dc5f7c0fd596f9f59f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791728602,"nonce":"Bc3mrRkkxOrmPHFuioXVktfR2w8=","title":"position: sticky","message":"position takes sticky beside static, relative, absolute and fixed. A sticky node is laid out as a relative one with none of its insets, and held top, right, bottom and left in from the edges of the view of its nearest scroll container, never out of its parent: its section's header stays in view and goes up with the section. The insets are kept in Tree::sticky, written through the router as before (they are read from the style for a node made sticky after them, and given back to it when it stops being sticky), and one function, Tree::sticky_shift, gives the shift to the paint walk, the hit test and the bounds, so what is seen is what is hit.\n\nNot done: the node still paints in tree order, so a later non-positioned sibling with a fill covers a held header; that is z-index's, which comes next. A transform between the node and its scroller is not accounted for in the hold.","files":null}]}